{"id": 34, "ts": 1779482165.72162, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I\u2019d like to add two observations from my interview notes about Candidate\u202fB that have not yet been shared. First, B demonstrated a relatively weak memory for numbers, which could affect tasks such as fuel calculations, performance data entry, and other numerically\u2011intensive cockpit duties\u2014an important aspect of decision quality and operational reliability. Second, B made several nasty remarks about his colleagues during the interview, indicating a potential problem with professional communication and crew cooperation.\nThese points temper B\u2019s strong reliability signals (very conscientious, handles stress well) and his technical strengths (weather assessment, computer skills). Compared with the other candidates, we still lack any concrete evidence of operational reliability for A and D, while A\u2019s interpersonal unfriendly behavior and D\u2019s hot temper also raise crew\u2011cooperation concerns. Candidate\u202fC shows calmness in crisis and solid technical understanding, but his communication difficulties and lack of willingness to pursue further education remain concerns.\nGiven the current evidence, B still appears to be the most balanced overall fit, provided we can address the memory and interpersonal issues\u2014perhaps through targeted training or a probationary period. I propose we keep Candidate\u202fB as the leading option while we consider whether B\u2019s memory limitation is acceptable for long\u2011distance operations.\nMETADATA_JSON:\n{\"agent\": \"agent_2\", \"vote\": \"Candidate B\"}"}
